Tests for this project

There are two kinds, run two different ways.

Pure-logic tests (on your PC)
------------------------------
Run with:

    pio test -e native

This runs on your own machine, not the ESP32 - no flashing, no hardware
needed, seconds instead of minutes.

Why only some code is tested this way
Almost everything in src/ talks directly to hardware (TFT_eSPI, Preferences/
NVS, analogRead, WiFi, LEDC), inline in the same functions as the actual
decision logic. That can't be unit tested without a lot of mocking, and
mocking a display/touchscreen/flash isn't worth it - the real thing gets
verified by actually running the device.

What's covered instead is logic that's easy to get subtly wrong and doesn't
need any hardware to check: given this input, is the output right? Each of
these lives in its own header/source pair with zero Arduino/ESP32 includes,
specifically so it can compile under `platform = native` in platformio.ini:

- AlarmLogic.h/.cpp - does a given alarm fire at a given moment (day-of-week,
  time-of-day, holiday skip, and the DST-hour correction)
- LdrHysteresis.h/.cpp - the backlight hysteresis/debounce decision
- WeatherIconShape.h/.cpp - weather condition string -> icon shape

Adding another one:
1. Pull the pure decision (no hardware calls) into its own .h/.cpp under
   include/ and src/, taking plain data in and returning plain data out.
2. Add it to the native env's build_src_filter in platformio.ini
   (+<YourFile.cpp>).
3. Add a test/test_<name>/test_<name>.cpp using Unity (see the existing
   suites for the pattern: setUp/tearDown, test_ functions, a main() that
   RUN_TESTs them).
4. The ESP32 build (env:esp32dev) picks up the new .cpp automatically -
   nothing to do there.

Hardware-in-the-loop tests (on the real device)
-------------------------------------------------
Run with:

    pio test -e esp32dev_test -f test_hardware --upload-port /dev/ttyACM0

(swap the port for whatever the device actually enumerates as - omit
--upload-port to let PlatformIO auto-detect if there's only one board
plugged in). This builds test/test_hardware, flashes it to the real ESP32,
and reads pass/fail back over serial - takes tens of seconds, not seconds,
and needs the board connected over USB.

The -f test_hardware filter matters: without it, `pio test -e esp32dev_test`
also tries to build the *native* suites above against this environment and
they fail to link (they use a plain main(), not Arduino's setup()/loop()).
That's an invocation mistake, not a real failure - always pass the filter
for this environment.

What's covered here: things that need real hardware and can't be tested
natively - an actual sensor pin (LDR), real flash storage (Preferences/NVS,
using its own "testStore" namespace so it never touches real alarms), and
real concurrent FreeRTOS tasks on real cores fighting over a mutex (a direct
regression test for the class of bug that caused the random reboots fixed by
DisplayLock - see git log for "serialize all access to the shared TFT_eSPI
display").

This is deliberately not comprehensive - it's not testing the display,
touchscreen, WiFi, or MQTT, because "does it work" for those is what
actually running the device already answers every time you flash it.
